The pyparsing development task now has a concrete public objective and a replay
of its reported defect. `public_issue_reproduction_v1/objective.json` freezes the
current public issue 633 body, its hash, authorized edit surface and pinned
pre-fix source. This is a new development objective; it does not alter any
historical request or establish the issue body's exact bytes at the base date.

The public report specifies a first numeric character followed by a colon and
`match_previous_expr(first)`. The expression should match `1:1` and reject
`1:2` and `1:a`, with default, nonrecursive and recursive whitespace handling.
The independently invoked public source reproduces all nine report examples:
eight conform to that expectation, while recursive whitespace incorrectly
allows `1:2`. The worker loads eleven actual pyparsing modules from the pinned
tree, and all 286 regular source files remain unchanged. The documentation
symlink is recorded as opaque metadata. No reference patch, hidden scorer or
provider is used. This establishes a reproducible public requirement, not
complete acceptance coverage or a new independent family.

The initial public-reference fetch remains in `public_task_authority_v1/`.
Click reference 2365 is a pull request, so its body was not admitted as an issue
objective. The original XML, OAuthlib and Click title/generic-hidden-acceptance
descriptions still require self-contained public specifications before live
integrated repair evaluation. Public source context qualification alone does
not fill that authority requirement.

Reproduction: `python evals/integrated_v1/qualify_public_issue.py` from the
revision root, in a fresh copy with no existing `public_issue_reproduction_v1`
directory. The script freezes its source, objective, source inventory and nine
expectations before execution, then retains raw output and source bindings.
